[recv] {"method":"item/completed","params":{"item":{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_00befeedb1b63f87016a2072e3bfbc81948ff7b97f57893117","text":"Implemented the tscircuit project in `/workspace`.\n\nMain files:\n- [index.circuit.tsx](/workspace/index.circuit.tsx)\n- [imports/RP2040.tsx](/workspace/imports/RP2040.tsx)\n\nWhat’s included:\n- JLCPCB-imported RP2040 component, no tscircuit registry components\n- USB-C port, 3.3 V regulator, flash, 12 MHz crystal, boot/reset buttons, SWD pins\n- GPIO breakout headers with a reduced minimal pinout\n- GPIO25 LED\n- Routing remains enabled; no `routingDisabled` or `--routing-disabled` usage\n\nValidation:\n- `npm run typecheck -- --pretty false`: passed\n- `tsci check netlist index.circuit.tsx`: passed\n- `tsci check placement index.circuit.tsx`: passed, 0 errors / 0 warnings\n- Full `tsci build index.circuit.tsx`: blocked by tscircuit autorouter. The default capacity autorouter repeatedly failed one node with `Exceeded max via count` / `MAX_RIPS`; `sequential_trace` was incompatible with the built-in USB-C connector footprint resolution.\n\n**TSCircuit Developer Feedback**\n- Confusing/surprising: `tsci build` exits code 0 even when it reports build errors, and “Port X is missing a trace” warnings appear broadly after an autorouter failure.\n- Most time wasted: debugging autorouter behavior after netlist and placement were already clean.\n- Helpful improvements: clearer mapping from `cmn_33` autorouter node back to actual nets/components; documented examples for autorouter presets with built-in USB-C.\n- Specific friction: `standard=\"usb_c\"` works with the default router but fails with `sequential_trace` because it reports no footprint; custom crystal footprint handling was also less discoverable than expected.","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null},"threadId":"019e8ead-3fe9-7073-8d25-1b277e826e16","turnId":"019e8ead-3ff4-7252-8829-239cc99569de","completedAtMs":1780511466745}}
